{"name":"Chinese Garden","hex":"#006967","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"garden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An outdoor area containing one or more types of plants, usually plants grown for food or ornamental purposes.","Such an ornamental place to which the public have access."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To grow plants in a garden; to create or maintain a garden.","Of a batsman, to inspect and tap the pitch lightly with the bat so as to smooth out small rough patches and irregularities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/garden"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chinese garden","blocks":[{"text":"The Chinese garden is a landscape garden style which has evolved over three thousand years. It includes both the vast gardens of the Chinese emperors and members of the imperial family, built for pleasure and to impress, and the more intimate gardens created by scholars, poets, former government officials, soldiers and merchants, made for reflection and escape from the outside world. They create an idealized miniature landscape, which is meant to express the harmony that should exist between man and nature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/20090510_Shanghai_Yuyan_6689.jpg/330px-20090510_Shanghai_Yuyan_6689.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_garden","wikidata":"Q47692"},{"title":"Garden","blocks":[{"text":"A garden is a planned space, usually outdoors, set aside for the cultivation, display, and enjoyment of plants and other forms of nature. The single feature identifying even the wildest wild garden is control. The garden can incorporate both natural and artificial materials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg/330px-Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden","wikidata":"Q1107656"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/YuanJiang-Penglai_Island.jpg/500px-YuanJiang-Penglai_Island.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/YuanJiang-Penglai_Island.jpg/500px-YuanJiang-Penglai_Island.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A miniature version of Mount Penglai, the legendary home of the Eight Immortals, was recreated in many classical Chinese gardens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYuanJiang-Penglai_Island.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Wang_Xizhi_by_Qian_Xuan.jpg/500px-Wang_Xizhi_by_Qian_Xuan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Wang_Xizhi_by_Qian_Xuan.jpg/1280px-Wang_Xizhi_by_Qian_Xuan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Wang_Xizhi_by_Qian_Xuan.jpg/500px-Wang_Xizhi_by_Qian_Xuan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The calligrapher Wang Xizhi in his garden, the Orchid Pavilion","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWang_Xizhi_by_Qian_Xuan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/%E5%AE%8B_%E9%87%91%E6%98%8E%E6%B1%A0%E7%88%AD%E6%A8%99%E5%9C%96.jpg/500px-%E5%AE%8B_%E9%87%91%E6%98%8E%E6%B1%A0%E7%88%AD%E6%A8%99%E5%9C%96.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/%E5%AE%8B_%E9%87%91%E6%98%8E%E6%B1%A0%E7%88%AD%E6%A8%99%E5%9C%96.jpg/1280px-%E5%AE%8B_%E9%87%91%E6%98%8E%E6%B1%A0%E7%88%AD%E6%A8%99%E5%9C%96.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=t